<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!DOCTYPE plist PUBLIC "-//Apple//DTD PLIST 1.0//EN" "http://www.apple.com/DTDs/PropertyList-1.0.dtd">
<plist version="1.0">
<dict>
	<!--
	  Dart AOT executables map the embedded snapshot as executable memory at
	  startup; under the hardened runtime that mapping is denied and the
	  process is killed (SIGKILL) before main() runs. This entitlement is the
	  documented minimum for Dart/Flutter AOT binaries.

	  Deliberately NOT granted (least privilege — this host handles vault
	  metadata):
	  - com.apple.security.cs.allow-jit: only relaxes MAP_JIT mappings; Dart
	    AOT does not JIT, so it does not fix the snapshot mapping and adds
	    nothing.
	  - com.apple.security.cs.disable-library-validation: the host loads no
	    third-party dylibs/plugins; leaving validation on prevents unsigned
	    code injection via library planting.
	-->
	<key>com.apple.security.cs.allow-unsigned-executable-memory</key>
	<true/>
	<!--
	  App-group membership by signature, NOT sandboxing (the host is not
	  sandboxed; application-groups on a non-sandboxed helper is the standard
	  Apple pattern for helpers sharing a group container).

	  Why: on macOS Sequoia, "App Data protection" TCC-guards group
	  containers. The native host is an ephemeral process (spawned per
	  browser request, <1s lifetime), and TCC's per-process grant does not
	  attribute reliably to short-lived processes — the "would like to
	  access data from other apps" prompt reappears on every spawn
	  (measured with the legacy non-prefixed group). Declaring the group in
	  the host's signature makes it a *member*, so TCC never asks — but
	  Sequoia only honors that membership when the group ID is prefixed
	  with the signing Team ID (measured: per-spawn prompts on
	  group.dev.camillobucciarelli.kdbxKeyVault, zero prompts on the
	  Team-prefixed group below across repeated third-party-parented
	  spawns). Hence the dedicated group, distinct from the legacy one the
	  app shares with the Apple autofill CredentialProviderExtension.

	  Least privilege: ONLY the browser-store group is declared. The host
	  must not be able to read the credential provider's container, so the
	  legacy group is deliberately absent.

	  Threat model unchanged: this grants the host nothing it did not
	  already have pre-Sequoia (any same-user non-sandboxed process could
	  read the store then). The store keeps 0700/0600 POSIX permissions,
	  the bearer in bridge.json is only usable with loopback access + the
	  triple binding (databaseId/cacheGeneration/bridgeGeneration) + an
	  unlocked vault + an authorized page origin, and the .kdbx vault is
	  never in this store. Full analysis on
	  DesktopBrowserAutofillCacheStore.defaultDirectory.
	-->
	<key>com.apple.security.application-groups</key>
	<array>
		<string>A8QUU5F9G3.dev.camillobucciarelli.kdbxKeyVault.browser</string>
	</array>
</dict>
</plist>
